ntally\n           for(int j=1; j<=n-s; j++){\n               int leftColSum = vPref[j-1][i+s-1] - (i>0?vPref[j-1][i-1]:0);\n               int rightColSum = vPref[j+s-1][i+s-1] - (i>0?vPref[j+s-1][i-1]:0);\n               curSum -= leftColSum;\n               curSum += rightColSum;\n               mxSum = max(mxSum, curSum);\n           }\n       }\n   }\n\nThis demonstrates work sharing via manual chunking (or we can use #pragma omp for schedule(static) on the outer i loop). We'll use #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static) directly.\n\nBut note that each iteration of i is independent and has O(s + n-s) = O(n) work. So static scheduling should be fine.\n\nWe need to compute vPref efficiently. Use vector<vector<int>> vPref(n, vector<int>(n)). Compute:\n   #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n   for(int j=0;j<n;j++){\n       int cum = 0;\n       for(int i=0;i<n;i++){\n           cum += matrix[i][j];\n           vPref[j][i] = cum; // store cumulative up to row i inclusive\n       }\n   }\n\nThis is O(N^2) and parallelized.\n\nMemory: matrix (n x n ints), vPref (n x n ints). That's 512 MB total. Might be high but acceptable? Possibly the system has enough memory. If not, we could compute column sums on the fly per thread using a shared array that is updated as i increments across threads? But that would require synchronization.\n\nGiven the goal of demonstrating OpenMP features, this approach uses work sharing (parallel for), reductions (max reduction), scheduling control (static schedule), and thread granularity via manual chunking or collapse if we use nested loops. We can also add collapse(2) on the i loop and j loop? But that would create many iterations; each iteration does constant work, so collapse could be beneficial.\n\nWe'll propose using #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(static) over both starting row i and starting column j. That gives (n-s+1)^2 iterations, each computing submatrix sum in O(s) t